    16	# Design analysis for combo D8/0 + D9/0
    17	# a) Concrete behavior trace.
    18	#    - 90-degree cell collision in the original solver: two cardinal rays try to enter the same cell,
    19	#      that cell is erased into a 1-pixel hole, then both rays turn toward the bisector and continue as
    20	#      two adjacent thin tracks. In this combo the turn stays the same, but the collision cell is painted.
    21	#      So the visible change is "same bend, no central hole".
    22	#    - 45-degree cell collision in the original solver: one cardinal ray claims the meeting cell, the
    23	#      diagonal ray steps sideways, and both heads continue with the same zigzag pattern. That creates a
    24	#      2-cell-thick staircase band. In this combo the diagonal ray merges instead of running in parallel:
    25	#      only one head survives, and the outgoing zigzag is a single-pixel staircase.
    26	#    - Small example: an upward ray and a north-east ray meeting at (r,c). Originally the output around
    27	#      the collision becomes a two-lane stair. Here only the upward ray keeps the collision cell and then
    28	#      follows the alternating (north-east, up) pattern, so the post-collision trace is one thin line.
    29	# b) Complexity calibration.
    30	#    - If the generator allowed no collisions, this combo would visually collapse back to the original
    31	#      input-output family. To keep both fused changes observable, every generated instance must contain
    32	#      at least one 90-degree cell collision and at least one 45-degree cell collision.
    33	#    - The generator therefore uses two explicit gadgets rather than unconstrained random placement.
    34	#      This keeps the task readable: humans see exactly two collision motifs, each repeated enough to
    35	#      infer "all collision cells are filled" and "zigzags never widen".
    36	# c) Hidden-assumption audit in main().
    37	#    - The original `blocked` set encoded the 90-degree gap. D9/0 invalidates that assumption, so the
    38	#      simulation can no longer treat those cells as permanently forbidden.
    39	#    - The original 45-degree branch assumed two synchronized heads must survive to model a thick band.
    40	#      D8/0 removes that assumption. The fused solver now intentionally deactivates the diagonal partner
    41	#      and keeps only one outgoing zigzag head.
    42	#    - Cross-collision and segment-cross logic do not depend on the removed gap/thickening cues, so they
    43	#      remain intact.
    44	# d) Placement legality.
    45	#    - Every frame is still a legal 4x4 border around a 2x2 monochrome block.
    46	#    - Frame top-left coordinates are derived from target collision cells and checked against the original
    47	#      `FRAME_BBOX_GAP` non-overlap rule. The chosen gadget geometry leaves all rays inside background
    48	#      space until the intended collision.
    49	# e) Visual observability.
    50	#    - Humans can read the rule from 2-4 examples because the same two collision motifs recur: a filled
    51	#      90-degree elbow and a thin 45-degree staircase. No color-id ordering or other invisible metadata is
    52	#      used to disambiguate outcomes.
    53	# f) Visual cleanliness.
    54	#    - The 90-degree gadget lives in the upper-right of the canvas and turns northwest.
    55	#    - The 45-degree gadget lives in the lower-left and turns southeast.
    56	#      This spatial split avoids clutter and makes the two modified outcomes easy to compare.
    57	# g) Inferability of color behavior.
    58	#    - Colors still only identify source blocks; the combo changes geometry, not recoloring. Each block
    59	#      keeps a unique beam color, so the solver can visually track which emitted line participated in which
    60	#      collision.
    61	# 9) New assumptions introduced by the combo solver.
    62	#    - The 45-degree merge now assumes the diagonal participant can be safely terminated at the collision;
    63	#      the generator enforces isolated 45-degree gadgets so that no later logic needs that diagonal head.
    64	#    - The 90-degree no-gap branch now paints the collision cell and lets one beam own it. The generator
    65	#      keeps later traffic away from that exact cell, avoiding ambiguous re-entry cases while still letting
    66	#      the outgoing turn be fully visible.
